    "case_id": "86",
    "case_summary": "A 47-year-old Caucasian male with a history of Diabetes Mellitus type I underwent simultaneous pancreas-kidney transplantation two years prior to the current admission. His past medical history also includes depression and a 20 pack-year smoking history, with cessation 10 years ago. Upon admission, he presented with a sore throat, progressive difficulty swallowing, painful swallowing, and weight loss. His immunosuppression regimen consisted of tacrolimus (8 mg/day) and prednisone (5 mg/day). Physical examination revealed a 4 cm exophytic lesion at the right base of the tongue extending to the inferior pole of the tonsillar fossa, along with significant right cervical lymphadenopathy. A computed tomographic (CT) scan of the neck showed an ill-defined mass in the right peripharyngeal region at the base of the tongue, causing deviation of the airway to the left. Additionally, a conglomerate mass involving lymph nodes invading the right sternocleidomastoid muscle was identified. A CT scan of the abdomen revealed enlarged right mesenteric lymph nodes and an area of thickening of the small bowel wall.\n\nThe hematoxylin-eosin (H&E) stained sections showed sheets of monotonous large atypical lymphoid cells with abundant cytoplasm, large vesicular nuclei and occasional prominent nucleoli. These neoplastic cells infiltrated the adjacent skeletal muscles of the pharynx. Mitotic figures were rare. Large foci of necrosis were noted. Immunohistochemical stains demonstrated that the large cells were positive for CD20, CD30, BCL2, CD38 and EBV latent membrane protein (LMP). CD5 was aberrantly expressed in these cells. These cells were negative for CD3 and CD79a. Staining for immunoglobulin light chains kappa and lambda revealed only a few scattered polyclonal plasma cells. Molecular studies revealed IGH gene rearrangement in the neoplastic cells.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
